TEST MATERIAL ONLY — NOT A CREDENTIAL, NOT A CA, NOT A DEPLOYMENT ARTEFACT.

  What      A self-signed X.509 certificate for the loopback address, used by
            test/harness/interceptor.ts to stand up an HTTPS origin on
            127.0.0.1 so the PRODUCTION outbound client can be observed
            (FR-75). It is presented to, and accepted by, exactly one client:
            a test process that has already set UNIFI_LOCAL_TLS_INSECURE=true.
  Subject   CN=127.0.0.1   SAN: IP:127.0.0.1   Issuer: itself (no CA)
  Validity  notBefore 2026-08-07, notAfter 2100-01-01 (far future, on purpose)
  Key       EC prime256v1; the matching private key is loopback-key.pem, which
            is committed beside it and is equally worthless.

  Why it is committed rather than generated at run time
            Node's standard library exposes crypto.generateKeyPairSync but NO
            X.509 certificate generation at all. selfsigned / node-forge /
            mkcert are new dev dependencies, which FR-75 forbids outright, and
            shelling out to `openssl` at test time is not portable to the
            windows-latest CI leg NFR-20 requires green. So the pair is minted
            once, out of band, and checked in. Do not spend an afternoon
            rediscovering this.

  Why it cannot rot
            The interceptor is reached only with UNIFI_LOCAL_TLS_INSECURE=true,
            under which UnifiClient.agentFor sets rejectUnauthorized:false —
            neither the expiry nor the SAN is verified. A certificate expiring
            in 2100 and one that expired yesterday behave identically here.
            There is no renewal task and no scheduled breakage.

  Regeneration command (run once, out of band; never run by a test)
            openssl req -x509 -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 \
              -nodes -keyout loopback-key.pem -out loopback-cert.pem \
              -days 26810 -subj "/CN=127.0.0.1" \
              -addext "subjectAltName=IP:127.0.0.1" \
              -addext "basicConstraints=critical,CA:FALSE" \
              -addext "keyUsage=critical,digitalSignature,keyEncipherment" \
              -addext "extendedKeyUsage=serverAuth"
            then restore this banner above the BEGIN line. PEM parsers skip
            every byte before the first -----BEGIN----- marker, which is what
            makes a banner possible at all.

  If a secret scanner flagged loopback-key.pem: it is correct that the file
  contains a private key and correct that this one is inert. It authenticates
  nothing but 127.0.0.1 inside this repository's test suite, it has never been
  presented to a real service, and it is the reason the outbound path can be
  tested offline. Allow-list this path rather than teaching people to wave
  private-key findings through.

